Chapter: HalPeripheralLinear
  cites Foreword chapter Board

 The peripheral lifecycle, done right. A UART is opened once, written
 twice, and closed once, threading the linear handle through each step.
 The handle is consumed and re-minted by every operation, so the
 exactly-once discipline holds across the whole sequence.

 The register writes are real memory-mapped stores (the base is inside
 mapped RAM), so this exercises the honest MMIO path, not a stub.

 The three adversarial counterparts are errors/hal-peripheral-leak,
 errors/hal-peripheral-double-release, and
 errors/hal-peripheral-use-after-close.

Section: Body
  talk : Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  talk (base) = act
    p0 <- uart-open base default-uart-config
    p1 <- uart-send p0 72
    p2 <- uart-send p1 73
    uart-close p2
  end

  opening : [Console, Device.Mmio] Nothing = act
    r <- talk 536872960
    print-line-uni ("uart lifecycle ok: " & show r)
  end
